        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "3d677c4b-10e7-5938-920f-ea1821f78025",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Ernst Haeckel, Richard Dawkins, Sam Harris, Christopher Hitchens, George Orwell\n- Publications: New Statesman, The Nation, The Weekly Standard, The Atlantic, London Review of Books, The Times Literary Supplement, Slate, Free Inquiry, Vanity Fair, Why Orwell Matters, Orwell's Victory\n- Concepts: Dysteleology, argument from poor design, dysteleological argument, science-oriented atheism\n\nFacts:\n- Dysteleology is an aggressive but optimistic form of science-oriented atheism.\n- Dysteleology was originally perhaps associated with Ernst Haeckel and his followers.\n- Dysteleology is now perhaps more associated with the atheism of Richard Dawkins, Sam Harris, and Christopher Hitchens.\n- Christopher Hitchens contributed to New Statesman, The Nation, The Weekly Standard, The Atlantic, London Review of Books, The Times Literary Supplement, Slate, Free Inquiry, and Vanity Fair.\n- The argument from poor design is also known as the dysteleological argument.\n- The argument from poor design argues against the existence of a creator God by reasoning that an omnipotent and omnibenevolent God would not create organisms with perceived suboptimal designs found in nature.\n- Why Orwell Matters is a book-length biographical essay by Christopher Hitchens.\n- Why Orwell Matters was released in the United Kingdom as Orwell's Victory.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
